WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.880
A door stands before you.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.494
There is no clear handle, so you push.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.100
Nothing.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.100
You pull.

5
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.900
It opens.

6
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.500
You feel foolish.

7
00:00:16.809 --> 00:00:18.426
But what if the problem was never you?

8
00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:20.009
What if it was the design?

9
00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:24.220
That tiny moment reveals the question
behind user experience:

10
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.864
can a product make sense to a human being?

11
00:00:28.464 --> 00:00:32.723
Long before apps, people struggled with
tools, machines, and everyday objects.

12
00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:34.673
During the twentieth century,

13
00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:41.211
industrial designers and human-factors
researchers began asking how controls
should fit human bodies and minds.

14
00:00:41.531 --> 00:00:45.002
In 1955, Henry Dreyfuss published
Designing for People,

15
00:00:45.052 --> 00:00:50.045
bringing measurements, observation, and
testing into the design conversation.

16
00:00:50.365 --> 00:00:54.070
Think telephone dials, cockpit switches,
and confusing appliances.

17
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.025
Good design was becoming less about the
machine alone,

18
00:00:57.075 --> 00:00:58.865
and more about the person using it.

19
00:01:00.465 --> 00:01:03.120
Early computers demanded that people adapt
to them.

20
00:01:03.445 --> 00:01:06.597
Instructions arrived through punched cards
or typed commands.

21
00:01:06.922 --> 00:01:09.116
Without knowing the language, you were
locked out.

22
00:01:09.721 --> 00:01:11.728
But researchers imagined something
different.

23
00:01:12.053 --> 00:01:15.228
What if information could be seen and
moved, not just commanded?

24
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:16.195
In 1963,

25
00:01:16.245 --> 00:01:21.615
Ivan Sutherland's Sketchpad made it
possible to draw directly on a computer
screen.

26
00:01:23.232 --> 00:01:23.675
In 1968,

27
00:01:23.725 --> 00:01:29.769
Douglas Engelbart and his team stunned an
audience with what we now call the Mother
of All Demos.

28
00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:33.374
They showed a mouse, linked documents,
on-screen editing,

29
00:01:33.424 --> 00:01:36.784
and collaboration, long before those ideas
felt ordinary.

30
00:01:37.137 --> 00:01:41.565
The mouse itself had emerged from
experiments earlier in the decade;

31
00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:43.788
a wooden prototype came in 1964.

32
00:01:44.141 --> 00:01:47.817
But the famous 1968 demonstration used a
later model.

33
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.285
Computing began to feel like something you
could point to, not simply program.

34
00:01:53.973 --> 00:01:58.308
In 1973, Xerox PARC built the Alto,
bringing graphical displays,

35
00:01:58.358 --> 00:02:02.057
windows, and the mouse into one
experimental machine.

36
00:02:02.377 --> 00:02:07.363
Xerox's Star followed in 1981 with a more
complete desktop metaphor.

37
00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:09.735
Apple's Lisa arrived in 1983,

38
00:02:09.785 --> 00:02:15.730
and the Macintosh in 1984 helped bring
graphical interaction to a wider audience.

39
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.160
Icons looked like familiar objects.

40
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.713
A folder held files.

41
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:21.233
A trash can meant delete.

42
00:02:21.606 --> 00:02:24.795
Complex systems borrowed the language of
everyday life.

43
00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:30.899
In 1988, Don Norman explained why everyday
objects confuse us.

44
00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:34.826
A year later, Tim Berners-Lee proposed the
World Wide Web at CERN.

45
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:39.398
By 1991, its first website made linked
information navigable.

46
00:02:39.902 --> 00:02:42.666
The question now reached beyond operating
a machine:

47
00:02:42.716 --> 00:02:46.233
could people find what they needed, and
feel confident doing it?

48
00:02:46.737 --> 00:02:51.957
In 1993, Norman helped establish 'user
experience' as a professional term at
Apple.

49
00:02:52.281 --> 00:02:54.599
That experience extended far beyond the
screen.

50
00:02:56.212 --> 00:02:59.076
As websites multiplied, navigation,
readability,

51
00:02:59.126 --> 00:03:02.526
trust, and speed began to matter as much
as appearance.

52
00:03:02.851 --> 00:03:05.265
Accessibility gained shared standards,

53
00:03:05.315 --> 00:03:09.593
including the first Web Content
Accessibility Guidelines in 1999.

54
00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:13.300
Then, in 2007, the iPhone made touching,
flicking,

55
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.926
and pinching central to a mainstream
mobile interface.

56
00:03:17.251 --> 00:03:17.669
In 2010,

57
00:03:17.719 --> 00:03:22.337
responsive web design offered a way for
layouts to adapt to different screens.

58
00:03:22.663 --> 00:03:26.379
The interface no longer belonged to one
desk or one kind of user.

59
00:03:27.994 --> 00:03:29.855
So what exactly are UX and UI?

60
00:03:30.381 --> 00:03:34.220
UI, or user interface, is the layer of
controls and feedback:

61
00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.968
buttons, labels, sounds, color, and
interaction patterns.

62
00:03:38.313 --> 00:03:42.721
UX, or user experience, is the broader
journey: can someone understand it,

63
00:03:42.771 --> 00:03:46.145
complete a task, trust the result, and
feel in control?

64
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.887
Imagine a stunning checkout page that
hides the delivery cost until the end.

65
00:03:51.313 --> 00:03:53.377
Beautiful UI; frustrating UX.

66
00:03:54.102 --> 00:03:56.692
Designers research real needs, map
journeys,

67
00:03:56.742 --> 00:04:00.462
sketch options, prototype interactions,
and test with people.

68
00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.007
Then they revise.

69
00:04:02.183 --> 00:04:04.498
Great experiences are not born from
guessing.

70
00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:06.325
They are improved by observing.

71
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:08.343
Today,

72
00:04:08.393 --> 00:04:13.708
designers collaborate and use shared
components and design systems to keep
products consistent.

73
00:04:14.061 --> 00:04:16.906
Browser-based tools allow teams to design
together.

74
00:04:17.438 --> 00:04:19.652
But progress is not just faster software.

75
00:04:20.004 --> 00:04:23.489
It is readable text, keyboard access,
clear error recovery,

76
00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:26.415
and choices that respect attention and
privacy.

77
00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:30.591
Standards such as WCAG 2.2 advance
inclusion.

78
00:04:31.123 --> 00:04:33.470
What counts is not how polished a screen
looks,

79
00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.815
but who it works for, and who it leaves
out.

80
00:04:37.523 --> 00:04:41.903
Now interfaces are changing again:
conversations with AI, voice commands,

81
00:04:41.953 --> 00:04:46.445
spatial computing, and experiences that
may not look like screens at all.

82
00:04:46.945 --> 00:04:48.145
The tools will change.

83
00:04:48.376 --> 00:04:49.805
The responsibility will not.

84
00:04:50.305 --> 00:04:51.572
Find the human problem.

85
00:04:51.892 --> 00:04:53.806
Design with people, not assumptions.

86
00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:55.326
Test what you have made.

87
00:04:55.781 --> 00:04:56.981
And remember that door?

88
00:04:57.311 --> 00:05:01.033
When it finally opens the way you expect,
you barely notice the design.

89
00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:02.633
That is often the point.

